// Vulnerable driver dropped
//
// Fires when ASR catches a known vulnerable signed driver being written to disk. Blocked
// means ASR stopped it. Audited means it went through. This is how a lot of EDR killers
// get in, and ASR doesn't raise an alert for it on its own.
//
// Runs:      Continuous (NRT)
// Severity:  High
// MITRE:     T1068, T1562.001
// Tables:    DeviceEvents
// Custom details: ActionType, FileName
//
// Needs:
//   - The ASR rule "Block abuse of exploited vulnerable signed drivers" in Audit or Block.
//
// Triage (paste into Recommended actions):
//   - Check who dropped it and from where.
//   - Escalate unless it's a documented hardware utility. Audited could be an EDR killer
//     being staged, so Tier 2 decides on isolation.
//   - Close if it's a documented utility, and ask the owner to remove it.
//
// Based on: Microsoft's ASR rules reference, https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/defender-endpoint/attack-surface-reduction-rules-reference

DeviceEvents
| where ingestion_time() > ago(1d)
| where ActionType in ("AsrVulnerableSignedDriverBlocked", "AsrVulnerableSignedDriverAudited")
| project Timestamp, ReportId, DeviceId, DeviceName, ActionType, FileName, FolderPath, SHA1, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, InitiatingProcessAccountName
